From 7179c4c13880411a3c3d0172ce97f2beec72aa13 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tsuji Riya <tsuji.riya@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 23:17:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/10] ARM: cnc1800l: bring up HDMI 1080p60 without U-Boot

The DSM-232 console relied on the vendor U-Boot "logo" command to set
up the display, and on a boot script fix for the AVI InfoFrame that
the vendor hdmi.bin gets wrong for 1080p. Set up the same 1920x1080p60
RGB565 scan-out of the simple-framebuffer at 0x0c000000 from Linux:
TVE PLL and dividers (power_clock.c clock_dispset_clockmoderaw()),
display feeder timing and graphics layer (df.c DFSetOutIFVideoFmt(),
CS_LoadDisplay()), then the Silicon Image HDMI transmitter with the
register writes of hdmi.bin recorded in an emulator, without audio and
HDCP, and with a correct AVI InfoFrame (VIC 16, BT.709, 16:9, YCbCr
4:4:4, underscan, IT content).

cnc1800l_display.skip=1 keeps what U-Boot set up instead.
cnc1800l_display.dump=1 prints the clock, pin mux, DF and HDMI TX
registers before (and after) the set-up, to compare with the state left
by U-Boot "logo".

On hardware, SYS_CTRL1 must keep HEN/VEN (0x37, as left by hdmi.bin)
and the DF graphics format keeps AlphaIsValid (0x12): with 0x07 the
transmitter measured no input timing and the TV got no signal.

cnc1800l_display.steps=<mask> runs only some of the set-up steps (clocks,
DF, HDMI block reset, HDMI TX registers) to bisect on hardware.
---
